def diagram(kind,caption,**kwargs):return {'type':'diagram','caption':caption,'diagram':{'type':kind,'title':caption,**kwargs}}
def plot(caption,poly,**kwargs):return diagram('source-function-graph',caption,polynomial=poly,xMin=-5,xMax=6,yMin=-10,yMax=15,xTicks=[-5,0,5],yTicks=[-10,-5,0,5,10],**kwargs)
FIG45=diagram('source-function-graph','Figure 4.5',polynomial=[-3,2,1],extraPolynomials=[[2,2]],xMin=-10,xMax=10,yMin=-10,yMax=15,xTicks=[-10,-5,0,5,10],yTicks=[-10,-5,0,5,10],points=[[-4,5,'(−4,5)'],[2,5,'(2,5)'],[-1,-4,'(−1,−4)'],[2.236,6.472,'(2.236,6.472)'],[-2.236,-2.472,'(−2.236,−2.472)']],annotations=[[5,11,'y'],[-4,-9,'y′']])
FIG47=diagram('source-critical-sign-pair','Figure 4.7',critical=[-1,3],poly=[1,-9,-3,1],points=[[-1,6,'(−1,6)'],[0,1,'(0,1)'],[3,-26,'(3,−26)']])
PAGES=[page('064_page_1.jpg',119,[
 heading('4.4 Maxima and Minima'),paragraph('Always the maximum and minimum values of a function can be read from its graphical view. For a quadratic function (whose graph is parabola), the maximum or minimum values can be determined without graphing by finding the vertex algebraically. For functions whose graphs are not known, other techniques are needed. In this unit, we shall see how to use derivatives to determine the maximum and minimum values of a function as well as the intervals where the function is increasing or decreasing.'),
 heading('4.4.1 Increasing and decreasing functions'),paragraph('Suppose an ecologist has determined the size of a population of a certain species as a function f(t) of time t (months). If it turns out that the population is increasing until the end of the first year and decreasing thereafter. It is reasonable to expect the population to be maximized at time t = 12 and for the population curve to have a high point at t = 12 as shown in Figure 4.3.'),diagram('source-population-curve','Figure 4.3'),
 paragraph('If the graph of a function f(t), such as this population curve, is rising throughout the interval 0 < t < 12, then we say that f(t) is strictly increasing on that interval. Similarly, the graph in Figure 4.3 is strictly decreasing on the interval 12 < t < 20. These terms are defined more formally in Figure 4.4.'),diagram('source-monotone-pair','Figure 4.4'),
 paragraph('The function f(x) is strictly increasing on an interval (a,b), if f(x₁) < f(x₂), whenever x₁ < x₂ for x₁ and x₂ on (a,b).'),paragraph('The function f(x) is strictly decreasing on an interval (a,b), if f(x₁) > f(x₂), whenever x₁ < x₂ for x₁ and x₂ on (a,b).'),
 heading('Example 22'),paragraph('Find the intervals at which the function f(x) = x² is increasing or decreasing.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('The function f(x) = x² is a parabola passing through the origin. Take any two points x₁,x₂ in the interval (a,b) for which:'),eq(r'f(x_2)-f(x_1)=x_2^2-x_1^2=(x_2-x_1)(x_2+x_1)'),
 paragraph('If x₁,x₂ ∈ (0,∞) with condition x₂ > x₁, then f(x) is increasing in (0,∞):'),eq(r'f(x_2)-f(x_1)>0\Rightarrow f(x_2)>f(x_1)'),paragraph('Both (x₂ − x₁) and (x₂ + x₁) are positive when x₂ > x₁.'),paragraph('If x₁,x₂ ∈ (−∞,0) with condition x₂ > x₁, then f(x) is decreasing in (−∞,0):'),eq(r'f(x_2)-f(x_1)<0\Rightarrow f(x_2)<f(x_1)'),paragraph('(x₂ − x₁) is positive while (x₂ + x₁) is negative when x₂ > x₁.')
]),page('064_page_2.jpg',120,[
 heading('4.4.2 Prove that if f(x) is a differentiable function on the open interval (a,b) then'),paragraph('f(x) is increasing on (a,b) if f′(x) > 0, ∀x ∈ (a,b). f(x) is decreasing on (a,b) if f′(x) < 0, ∀x ∈ (a,b).'),heading('Proof'),paragraph('Let x₁,x₂ ∈ (a,b) such that x₂ > x₁; then there exists a point c between x₁ and x₂ such that:'),eq(r'f′(c)=\frac{f(x_2)-f(x_1)}{x_2-x_1}'),eq(r'(x_2-x_1)f′(c)=f(x_2)-f(x_1)'),paragraph('For f′(c) > 0 and so x₂ − x₁ > 0. Therefore:'),eq(r'f(x_2)-f(x_1)>0\quadif\quadx_2>x_1'),eq(r'f(x_2)>f(x_1)\quadif\quadx_2>x_1'),paragraph('Thus, f is an increasing function. Similarly, the proof of part (ii) can be done which is left as an exercise for the reader.'),
 heading('Remember'),paragraph('If a function f is continuous on [a,b] and differentiable on (a,b), then there exists a point c ∈ (a,b) such that:'),eq(r'\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}=f′(c)'),
 note('For the decreasing case, x₂ − x₁ > 0 and f′(c) < 0 imply f(x₂) − f(x₁) < 0, hence f(x₂) < f(x₁). Differentiability on (a,b) supplies continuity on the smaller closed interval [x₁,x₂], so the mean value theorem applies.'),
 heading('Example 23'),paragraph('Determine the values of x at which f(x) = x² + 2x − 3 is increasing or decreasing. Also find the point at which the function is neither increasing nor decreasing.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('For graphical view, the given function through completing square:'),eq(r'f(x)=x^2+2x-3=x^2+2x+1-1-3=(x+1)^2-4'),
 paragraph('is compared with the general equation of parabola f(x) = a(x − h)² + k to obtain a parabola with vertex (−1,−4) that opens upward (a = 1 is positive). The graph through points (−4,5) and (2,5) is shown in Figure 4.5.'),FIG45,
 paragraph('The derivative with respect to x is the slope of the parabola:'),eq(r'f′(x)=2x+2'),paragraph('If the slope is positive, then:'),eq(r'f′(x)>0\Rightarrow2x+2>0\Rightarrow2x>-2\Rightarrow x>-1'),paragraph('The function is increasing in (−1,∞). If the slope is negative:'),eq(r'f′(x)<0\Rightarrow2x+2<0\Rightarrow2x<-2\Rightarrow x<-1'),paragraph('The function is decreasing in (−∞,−1). If the slope is zero:'),eq(r'f′(x)=0\Rightarrow2x+2=0\Rightarrow2x=-2\Rightarrow x=-1'),paragraph('The given function is neither increasing nor decreasing at the vertex (−1,−4).'),
 heading('4.4.3 Examination of a given function for extreme values'),paragraph('Typically the extrema of a continuous function occur either at endpoints of the interval or at points where the graph has a “peak” or “valley” (points where the graph is higher or lower than all nearby points). For example, f(x) in Figure 4.6 has peaks at B and D and valleys at C and E. Peaks and valleys are what we call the relative extrema.'),paragraph('The exact location of a relative maximum or minimum rather than a graphical approximation can normally be found by using derivatives. The concept developed is as under:'),paragraph('Let f(x) be a function as a roller coaster track with a roller coaster car moving from left to right along the graph in Figure 4.6. As the car moves up towards a peak, its floor tilts upward. At the'),
 note('Increasing and decreasing describe behavior on intervals. At the vertex in Example 23, the derivative is zero and the function changes from decreasing to increasing; “neither” refers to its zero instantaneous slope. The roller-coaster explanation continues on page 121.')
]),page('065_page_1.jpg',121,[
 heading('Roller-coaster analogy — continued'),paragraph('instant the car reaches the peak, its floor is level, but then it begins to tilt downward as the car rolls down toward a valley. At any point along the graph, the floor of the car (a straight-line segment in the figure) represents the tangent line to the graph at that point. Using this analogy, we see that as the car passes through the peaks and valleys at A,B,C, the tangent line is horizontal and has slope 0. At peak D and valley E, however, a real roller coaster car would have trouble. It would fly off the track at peak D and be unable to make the 90° change of direction at valley E. There is no tangent line at D or E because of the sharp corners.'),paragraph('Thus, the points where a peak or a valley occurs are the points where the tangent line is horizontal and has slope 0 or where no tangent line is defined. The slope of the tangent line to the graph of f(x) at P(x,f(x)) is the value of f′(x).'),diagram('source-roller-extrema','Figure 4.6'),
 note('The source text refers to peaks/valleys at A,B,C, but A is the left endpoint in the drawing. The diagram prints “No tangent” beside A,B,D,E although B is drawn as a smooth peak; these labels are preserved as printed. Smooth interior extrema have zero derivative; corners may have no finite derivative. Endpoint classification must use the chosen domain.'),
 heading('A. Relative Maximum and Relative Minimum'),paragraph('The function f(x) is said to have a relative maximum at a number c if f(c) ≥ f(x) for all x in an open interval containing c. Also, it is said to have a relative minimum at a number d if f(d) ≤ f(x) for all x in an open interval containing d. In general, the relative maxima and relative minima are called relative extrema.'),
 heading('B. Critical Value and Critical Point'),paragraph('Suppose f(x) is defined at a number c and either f′(c) = 0 or f′(c) does not exist. Then c is called a critical value of f(x), and P(c,f(c)) on the graph is called a critical point. If f(c) is not defined, then c cannot be a critical value. If there is a relative maximum at c, f(c) is the maximum value; similarly f(c) is the minimum value at a relative minimum.'),
 heading('Example 24'),paragraph(r'Find the critical values: (a) f(x) = 4x³ − 5x² − 8x + 20; (b) \(f(x)=\frac{x^2}{x-2}\); (c) \(f(x)=12x^{1/2}-2x^{3/2}\); (d) \(f(x)=3x^{4/3}-12x^{1/3}\); (e) \(f(x)=6x^{2/3}-4x\).'),heading('Solution'),
 paragraph('a. The first derivative is:'),eq(r'f′(x)=12x^2-10x-8'),paragraph('It is defined for all x. Set f′(x) = 0:'),eq(r'12x^2-10x-8=0\Rightarrow2(3x-4)(2x+1)=0\Rightarrow x=\frac43,-\frac12'),
 paragraph('b. The first derivative is:'),eq(r'f′(x)=\frac{x(x-4)}{(x-2)^2}'),paragraph('The derivative is not defined at x = 2, and the original function is not defined at x = 2. So x = 2 is not a critical value. Set f′(x) = 0:'),eq(r'\frac{x(x-4)}{(x-2)^2}=0\Rightarrow x=0,4'),paragraph('Parts (c)–(e) continue on page 122.')
]),page('065_page_2.jpg',122,[
 heading('Example 24 — continued'),paragraph('c. The first derivative is:'),eq(r'f′(x)=6x^{-1/2}-3x^{1/2}'),paragraph('The derivative is not defined at x = 0, but the original function f(x) is defined at x = 0: f(0) = 12(0)¹/² − 2(0)³/² = 0. So x = 0 is a critical value. For other values, set f′(x) = 0:'),eq(r'6x^{-1/2}-3x^{1/2}=0\Rightarrow3x^{-1/2}(2-x)=0\Rightarrow x=2'),paragraph('Thus, the critical values are x = 0,2.'),
 paragraph('d. The derivative is:'),eq(r'f(x)=3x^{4/3}-12x^{1/3}'),eq(r'f′(x)=3\left(\frac43\right)x^{4/3-1}-12\left(\frac13\right)x^{1/3-1}=4x^{1/3}-4x^{-2/3}=4x^{1/3}-\frac4{x^{2/3}}=\frac{4x-4}{x^{2/3}}'),paragraph('The derivative fails to exist when x = 0, but the original function is defined at x = 0, so x = 0 is a critical value. If x ≠ 0, f′(x) is zero only when 4x − 4 = 0, or x = 1. Thus the critical values are 0 and 1.'),
 paragraph('e. The derivative is:'),eq(r'f(x)=6x^{2/3}-4x'),eq(r'f′(x)=6\left(\frac23\right)x^{2/3-1}-4=4x^{-1/3}-4=\frac4{x^{1/3}}-4=\frac{-4-4x^{1/3}}{x^{1/3}}'),paragraph('The derivative fails to exist when x = 0, but f(x) is defined when x = 0, so x = 0 is a critical value. If x ≠ 0, f′(x) is zero only when the numerator 4 − 4x¹/³ is zero, for x = 1. Thus the critical values are 0 and 1.'),
 note('The last printed fraction in (e) changes 4 to −4 in its numerator. The correct fraction is (4 − 4∛x)/∛x, consistent with the next sentence and critical value 1. In (d),(e), fractional thirds denote real cube roots, including negative x. In (c), the real domain is x ≥ 0; 0 is a source-defined critical endpoint with no finite standard derivative.'),
 heading('Theorem 4.1'),paragraph('If a continuous function f(x) has a relative extremum at c, then c must be a critical value of f(x).'),note('This statement uses the source definition that includes points where the derivative does not exist. For an interior local extremum where a derivative exists, Fermat’s theorem gives f′(c) = 0. Endpoint extrema require domain-aware treatment.'),
 heading('Example 25'),paragraph('The function f(x) is defined by f(x) = x³ − 3x² − 9x + 1. Determine the intervals at which the function is strictly increasing or decreasing.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('First, find the derivative:'),eq(r'f′(x)=3x^2-6x-9'),paragraph('For critical values set f′(x) = 0:'),eq(r'3x^2-6x-9=0\Rightarrow3(x+1)(x-3)=0\Rightarrow x=-1,3'),paragraph('These critical values divide the x-axis into three parts, as shown in Figure 4.7. Select a typical number from each interval; for example −2,0,4, evaluate the derivative and mark each interval as increasing or decreasing according to whether the derivative is positive or negative respectively.'),FIG47,
 paragraph('Thus, f(x) increases for x < −1 and x > 3, but decreases in −1 < x < 3.')
])]
